---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Faithfulness Judge · San Lee"
  description="Can an LLM judge catch a made-up claim? 193 hand-labeled claims over public defense text, and the max_tokens bug that invented a 0.70 versus 0.43 tier gap."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/faithfulness-judge.html"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/assets/og-faithfulness-judge.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Claim-vs-source plate (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: the unit of the
       study, not a metrics chart (the κ table already carries the numbers).
       Max-width 260 keeps viewBox units 1:1 with CSS px on every gate width
       (same measured discipline as .two-fail / .jtbd-strip / .lab-topo). */
    .judge-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .judge-plate .jp-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .judge-plate .jp-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .judge-plate .jp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .judge-plate .jp-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 8px;
    }
    /* Schematic passage marks — not a real quote, not UI chrome. */
    .judge-plate .jp-glyph {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.25;
      stroke-linecap: round;
    }
    .judge-plate .jp-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Supported: ink solid — the ordinary pass. Dual-encoded by word + stroke. */
    .judge-plate .jp-ok {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .judge-plate .jp-ok-text {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    /* Unsupported: oxide — the class the study is built to catch. Word + oxide
       dual-encode; not colour-only. Oxide is for the negative finding, same
       as .two-fail FAIL (not a "red means bad UI"). */
    .judge-plate .jp-bad {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .judge-plate .jp-bad-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .judge-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .judge-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0 0 6px;
    }

    /* Truncation plate: the false gap. Cheap tier cut before the verdict
       word; scoring counted each empty as a miss. Oxide on the false gap. */
    .trunc-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-hollow {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 8px;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-edge-break {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-bad {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .trunc-plate .tp-bad-text {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .trunc-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .trunc-plate marker path.tp-head-bad { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    .trunc-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0 0 18px;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="faithfulness-judge" />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="../index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Faithfulness Judge</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Can an LLM judge catch a made-up claim?</p>

    <p class="lead">
      Every LLM system I build is graded by another LLM at some point. This
      project measures the grader. It takes answers generated over public
      defense text, breaks them into individual claims, has a person label each
      one against its cited source, and then asks two model tiers to do the same
      job blind. The deliverable is a number for how far the judge can be
      trusted, and where it cannot.
    </p>

    <!-- Unit of the study: one claim read against its cited source, labeled
         supported or unsupported. Not a κ chart — the metrics table below
         already has the numbers. Schematic only; no invented sample text as
         a real quote, no fake screenshots. ADR-010 N/A — no interactive nodes. -->
    <svg class="judge-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 132" role="img"
         aria-label="The unit of the faithfulness study: a claim is read against its cited source and labeled supported or unsupported. Source passage flows into a claim sentence; the binary verdict is supported or unsupported.">
      <title>The unit of the faithfulness study: a claim is read against its cited source and labeled supported or unsupported. Source passage flows into a claim sentence; the binary verdict is supported or unsupported.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="jp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- Top row: Source → Claim. Glyph lines stand for a passage, not a real quote. -->
      <text class="jp-lane" x="4" y="12">SOURCE</text>
      <rect class="jp-box" x="4" y="18" width="100" height="48" rx="2" />
      <g class="jp-glyph" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M 16 32 H 90" />
        <path d="M 16 40 H 82" />
        <path d="M 16 48 H 70" />
      </g>
      <text class="jp-micro" x="54" y="60" text-anchor="middle">cited passage</text>

      <path class="jp-edge" d="M 106 42 L 122 42" marker-end="url(#jp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

      <text class="jp-lane" x="126" y="12">CLAIM</text>
      <rect class="jp-box" x="126" y="18" width="130" height="48" rx="2" />
      <text class="jp-title" x="191" y="42" text-anchor="middle">asserted sentence</text>
      <text class="jp-micro" x="191" y="58" text-anchor="middle">one gold unit</text>

      <!-- Bottom row: binary verdict under the pair. Word + stroke dual-encode;
           oxide only on unsupported (the class the study is built to catch). -->
      <path class="jp-edge" d="M 130 68 L 130 84" marker-end="url(#jp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <text class="jp-lane" x="4" y="100">VERDICT</text>
      <rect class="jp-ok" x="4" y="106" width="122" height="22" rx="2" />
      <text class="jp-ok-text" x="65" y="121" text-anchor="middle">SUPPORTED</text>
      <rect class="jp-bad" x="134" y="106" width="122" height="22" rx="2" />
      <text class="jp-bad-text" x="195" y="121" text-anchor="middle">UNSUPPORTED</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="judge-plate-caption">
      One claim, one cited source, one binary label. The table below measures
      how often the model judge matches the human gold on that unit.
    </p>

    <h2>The result</h2>
    <table class="metrics">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Judge</th><th>Binary &kappa;</th><th>Raw agreement (95% CI)</th><th>Unsupported recall</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Opus</td><td>0.762</td><td>89.9% [84.8, 93.5]</td><td>97.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sonnet</td><td>0.716</td><td>88.4% [83.0, 92.2]</td><td>89.6%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Both tiers land in what the statistics literature calls substantial
      agreement. Either is usable as an automated faithfulness check. The
      premium tier edges the cheap one on &kappa;, and the confidence intervals
      overlap at this sample size. The gap is not resolvable from this data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those figures are measured against a gold set I audited in August, blind,
      under a selection rule committed before I looked at a single label.
      <strong>28 of the 30 claims it pulled came back unchanged</strong>. That
      consistency is the finding. The audit also moved Opus from 0.751 to 0.762.
      Restricted to the corrections that landed on claims neither judge had
      gotten wrong, it is 0.752, flat. The audit had reached one claim both
      judges failed, and removing it lifts agreement arithmetically. The check
      was pre-registered. It is
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge/blob/main/decisions/002-solid-tier-call.md#phase-2-result-2026-08-02">published both ways</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unsupported recall is <strong>97.9% versus 89.6%</strong>. Those
      percentages are 47 versus 43 catches out of 48, and that 48 folds in
      hedged half-claims alongside outright fabrications. Restrict it to
      fabrication proper, the 36 claims the source flatly does not back, and the
      two tiers are <strong>identical</strong>: 35 of 36 each, missing the same
      claim. All four of the disagreeing pairs behind the gap are hedged claims,
      which the rubric itself calls the least crisp call a labeler makes.
      McNemar&rsquo;s exact test on those four gives
      <strong>p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.125</strong>: the direction is consistent, with no
      reversals in 48 chances, but four pairs cannot size an effect. An earlier
      version of this page reported that gap as the reason to pay for the
      premium tier, and called it recall on the fabrication class. The test and
      the restriction are the two corrections.
    </p>
    <p>
      So <strong>neither axis separates the tiers on this set</strong>. Paired
      across all 189 claims, the premium judge is right where the cheap one is
      wrong six times and wrong where it&rsquo;s right three times, which is
      <strong>p&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.51</strong>. The cheap tier is good enough and
      escalation is not evidenced.
    </p>

    <h2>The result I almost published instead</h2>

    <svg class="trunc-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 132" role="img"
         aria-label="A token cap cut the cheap judge before its verdict on 20 percent of claims. Scoring counted each empty as a disagreement, which invented a tier gap of 0.70 versus 0.43. The gap was a harness bug.">
      <title>max_tokens cut the cheap tier before the verdict. The 0.70 vs 0.43 gap was a bug.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="tp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <marker id="tp-arrow-bad" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path class="tp-head-bad" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <text class="tp-lane" x="4" y="12">PREMIUM</text>
      <rect class="tp-box" x="4" y="18" width="110" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="tp-title" x="59" y="40" text-anchor="middle">verdict word</text>
      <text class="tp-micro" x="59" y="68" text-anchor="middle">finished</text>
      <text class="tp-lane" x="146" y="12">CHEAP TIER</text>
      <rect class="tp-box" x="146" y="18" width="50" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="tp-micro" x="171" y="40" text-anchor="middle">reason</text>
      <path class="tp-edge-break" d="M 196 36 L 206 36" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="tp-edge-break" d="M 214 36 L 224 36" marker-end="url(#tp-arrow-bad)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <rect class="tp-hollow" x="226" y="18" width="30" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="tp-lane" x="4" y="92">SCORED AS</text>
      <rect class="tp-bad" x="4" y="98" width="252" height="26" rx="2" />
      <text class="tp-bad-text" x="130" y="115" text-anchor="middle">FALSE GAP  0.70 vs 0.43</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="trunc-plate-caption">
      A token cap cut the cheap tier before the verdict on 20% of the set.
      Scoring counted each empty as a miss. The gap was my harness.
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">A truncation setting that looked like a finding</p>
      <p>The first scoring run showed Opus at 0.70 and Sonnet at
        <strong>0.43</strong>. A clean tier gap. It was one commit from
        shipping.</p>
      <p>It was false. I had set <code>max_tokens=10</code> to force a one-word
        verdict out of the judge. Opus complied. Sonnet tended to open with a
        short clause of reasoning first, so on <strong>39 of the 191 claims
        then scored</strong>,
        a full 20%, it was cut off before it ever reached the verdict word. My
        scoring counted every unparsed verdict as a disagreement, so a fifth of
        Sonnet&rsquo;s answers were scored as wrong when they were simply
        missing. On the 152 claims it actually finished, Sonnet was already at
        about 0.70, which is where it sits today.</p>
      <p>What caught it was reading the misjudgment log before publishing.
        Sonnet&rsquo;s failures did not look like judgment errors, which are
        scattered and arguable. They looked <em>empty</em>.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The lesson:</strong> inspect the raw outputs
        before believing a surprising metric. A number can be reproducible and
        still measure the harness. Constrain a critical output
        <em>structurally</em>, not with a token budget.</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The fix took two tries. Prefilling the assistant&rsquo;s reply, seeding
      the response so the model continues from a fixed token, is the standard
      trick for forcing a format, and these models reject it. What works is a
      forced tool call: a <code>record_verdict</code> tool whose schema
      constrains the verdict to the label set, with the tool choice pinned so
      the model has to use it. The model can reason as long as it likes, and the
      verdict arrives in a structured block that cannot be truncated into
      ambiguity. That is the same pattern the classifier uses to guarantee
      valid labels.
    </p>
    <p>
      Counting unparsed verdicts as disagreements is harsh. Dropping them would
      have discarded 20% of the set and hidden the truncation. The strict rule
      is what made the bug visible. Full decision record in
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge/blob/main/decisions/001-both-tiers-substantial.md">ADR-001</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What this grades against</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="defense-news-classifier.html">classifier</a> is graded
      against cheap, objective labels: a story is about procurement or it is
      not, and two careful people will mostly agree. This project grades against
      expensive, subjective ones. Deciding whether a specific sentence is
      supported by a specific cited passage takes real reading. The boundaries
      are arguable, and a hedged half-claim is a judgment call. It reuses the
      classifier&rsquo;s machinery, the judge harness, the Wilson intervals, the
      gold-set discipline, and points it at the opposite ground-truth regime.
    </p>
    <p>
      The classifier retired BM25 grounding after measuring it, then declined
      tiered model routing after measuring that. Here the cheap tier is again
      close enough that escalation is hard to justify on overall agreement.
      Three independent measurements land on <em>escalation barely pays</em>.
      That is why the default across this system is the cheaper model until an
      eval says otherwise.
    </p>

    <h2>What this number does not establish</h2>
    <p>
      The limits, also stated on the repo&rsquo;s front page:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One labeler.</strong> The gold set is my labels alone, with no
        inter-annotator agreement measured. That makes the ground truth one
        person&rsquo;s consistent reading of a written rubric, not a validated
        consensus. A judge agreeing with this gold at 0.762 has not been shown
        to agree with humans in general at 0.762, and adding a second labeler is
        the single thing that would upgrade the claim. <strong>The August audit
        does not change this.</strong> It was the same person re-reading his own
        labels, blind to the verdicts, which measures whether the gold is
        self-consistent and cannot measure whether it is right. A consistency
        audit is never inter-annotator agreement.</li>
      <li><strong>The gold set has been audited twice, and moved both times.</strong>
        The first pass caught two claims labeled supported that were filler with
        no factual assertion, which the rubric excludes. The second, the blind
        one, found a third instance the first pass had missed &mdash; and
        reversed one of the first pass&rsquo;s own calls, where the claim does
        assert that the source omits a detail and so is a correct refusal rather
        than filler. The second audit is also the evidence that the first was
        incomplete.</li>
      <li><strong>189 scored claims</strong> out of 193, with four excluded as
        non-claims. The intervals are about nine points wide. Any difference
        smaller than that is noise, and that includes both the tier gap in
        &kappa; and the recall gap, whose denominator is only 48.</li>
      <li><strong>Floor tier by design.</strong> One pass, no judge prompt
        tuning, no ensembles, no retrieval. This measures the ruler as built,
        not the best ruler obtainable.</li>
      <li><strong>Short passages from one domain.</strong> Public defense
        reporting, skewed toward operations and procurement. Faithfulness
        judging over long documents or technical specifications is untested
        here.</li>
      <li><strong>A floor:</strong> below about 0.6 I would not trust an
        automated verdict unreviewed. Both judges clear that. Neither clears the
        bar for treating a verdict as final without a human in the loop on
        anything consequential.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Python, the Anthropic SDK (Sonnet and Opus as the two judges under test),
      forced tool-use for the verdict schema, Cohen&rsquo;s &kappa; and Wilson
      intervals computed directly, pytest on mocked calls. Source text is public
      DVIDS reporting; nothing proprietary or non-public is used anywhere in the
      project. The repo is public:
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge">github.com/sanlee-ys/faithfulness-judge</a>.
      The gold set, the labeling guide, the misjudgment log, and the ADR that
      documents the bug are checkable.
    </p>

    <h2>What it demonstrates</h2>
    <p>
      The first scoring run printed a dramatic tier gap. The misjudgment log
      showed the cheap tier had been cut off. The published result is the one
      that survived that check: both judges are good enough, and escalation is
      not evidenced.
    </p>
  </main>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/last-updated.js"></script>
</Base>
